  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 237 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The Nor'easter to our south continues to be the focus of \nthe forecast as we remain on the periphery of the system. Throughout \nthe day today, several rounds of showers are expected to move \nthrough, with more measurable precipitation in comparison to \nyesterday. Total rainfall amounts associated with this system will \nvary greatly depending on location, with 1 to 1.5 inches expected \nacross southern Vermont by Monday afternoon while northern New York \nwill likely only see a few hundredths in the same time period. Given \nthe long duration of this event, rain will be largely beneficial \nwith no hydrological concerns. Shower activity will gradually taper \noff Monday as the low shifts eastward, with drier weather \nanticipated for Tuesday and Wednesday. In addition to the \nprecipitation, breezy winds will continue across the region this \nmorning given the strong pressure gradient, although the winds look \nto gradually diminish throughout the day as the pressure gradient \nweakens with winds trending light by Monday night. Gusts this \nafternoon looks to generally be in the 15 to 25 mph range, with the \ngreatest concern across mountain summits. Temperatures will be on \nthe cooler side as well, especially for Sunday and Monday with \nclouds and periods of showers expected, especially across southern \nand eastern Vermont.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A narrow 700-500mb ridge axis brings a tranquil start to \nthe extended forecast period Tuesday night and Wednesday with light \nwinds and slightly above normal temperatures. Any lingering low\nclouds should dissipate with increased subsidence. Highs on \nWednesday and Thursday should reach the low-mid 70s in most \nvalley locations. A modest cold front will bring a period of \nmainly light rain showers Thursday night into Friday morning. \nMax PoPs for this system currently around 50 percent, and \nrainfall amounts are generally expected to remain below 0.25\". \nGusty NW winds following frontal passage ushers in cool and dry \nconditions later Friday and into Saturday. With 850mb \ntemperatures near 0C Saturday afternoon, daytime highs will be \nsignificantly cooler to start the weekend, with highs generally \nin the 50s.",
